4. Contractor records linked to assigned work
Project teams need to understand which contractor is responsible for each activity and which workers have the required induction, competence and authorisation. These are related checks, but they are not interchangeable.

For example, completing a general site induction does not establish competence for a specialist task. Digital records should help reviewers make that distinction and identify records requiring renewal or further review.

5. Incident and near-miss reporting that supports learning
A near miss at one station can reveal a problem relevant to another. Structured reporting makes it easier to compare locations, understand contributing factors and track the resulting actions.

The purpose is not simply to collect more reports. It is to make reporting useful to the people doing the work. A worker who reports a problem should be able to learn what happened next. NeoEHS Incident Management connects reporting with investigation and corrective-action workflows.

6. Environmental and occupational-health follow-up
EHS records should cover environmental and health concerns alongside immediate safety hazards. Depending on the work, this may include dust, noise, vibration, heat exposure, chemicals, waste handling and drainage.

Monitoring results need context: where a measurement was taken, when, under what conditions and what response followed. Applicable limits and obligations depend on the project and jurisdiction. Sensitive personal health information also needs appropriately restricted access.

Where AI adds practical value
AI is most useful when it helps reviewers understand information they already struggle to process. Potential applications include grouping similar observations, identifying repeated findings across work packages and highlighting records that need further attention.

For example, several reports describing blocked access routes may use different wording. AI-assisted analysis can help a reviewer identify the common theme and investigate whether it reflects a wider planning issue.

Computer vision can provide an additional monitoring input for selected use cases. Its performance depends on factors such as camera coverage, lighting, visibility and validation in the actual environment. See NeoEHS AI-Powered Computer Vision.

AI outputs require review. A risk score is not proof that a task is safe, and an alert is not a substitute for an effective response. Project teams should establish who reviews alerts, how false alarms are handled and what happens when an important condition is missed.

A practical example: one finding, several affected teams
The following is an illustrative workflow, not a reported customer result.

During a depot inspection, a supervisor finds materials obstructing a designated access route. Mechanical and electrical contractors both use the area, and another shift is due to start shortly.

The supervisor records the exact location, photographs and affected work area.
The responsible team reviews the issue and puts the necessary immediate controls in place.
An action is assigned to a named owner, with affected supervisors notified.
The owner records completion and supporting evidence.
An authorised reviewer checks that the route is suitable before closing the action.
The incoming shift receives any remaining restrictions, and management reviews repeated access-route findings across the project.
The benefit is a visible chain of responsibility. Everyone can distinguish a reported issue, an action underway and a verified resolution.

Which EHS indicators should project leaders review?
A useful dashboard connects numbers to decisions. The following measures can help teams identify where follow-up is needed:

Indicator	What to examine
Overdue high-priority actions	Severity, age, location, owner and escalation status
Inspection completion	Completed versus scheduled inspections, with risk-based coverage
Verified closure rate	Actions verified as effective divided by actions submitted for closure during the reporting period
Repeated findings	Similar issues recurring after earlier action, using a consistent classification
Competency gaps	Missing or expired requirements relevant to the people and work assigned
Handover completeness	Whether open issues, restrictions and responsible owners are recorded and acknowledged
Agree definitions and reporting periods before comparing results. A higher near-miss reporting count can reflect greater willingness to report; it should not automatically be treated as worsening safety. Differences in work exposure and project phase also affect comparisons.

How to introduce digital EHS without overwhelming the workforce
Start with a workflow that has a clear operational problem, such as inspection findings that remain open across shifts. Map how the process works today, who makes decisions and where information is lost.

Configure a consistent location and contractor structure, a short set of essential fields and clear responsibilities. Pilot the workflow on a representative work package, including the field conditions in which people will actually use it.

Check mobile usability, permissions, evidence quality and behaviour during poor connectivity. Where offline functionality is required, test exactly which activities are supported and how synchronisation conflicts are handled.

Expand once the pilot demonstrates that people can report an issue, act on it and verify the outcome. Add further workflows and AI use cases as the underlying information becomes reliable.

