Frequently asked questions
What is the difference between digital EHS and a railway signalling system?
Digital EHS manages workflows such as inspections, reporting, permits and corrective actions. Signalling and train-protection systems manage railway movement and operational protection. Their purposes are distinct; EHS software does not replace those safety-critical systems.

Can one EHS platform cover construction and railway operations?
It can support both when workflows, permissions and records are configured for each phase. Handover needs particular attention so that residual risks, restrictions and open actions retain clear ownership.

Can AI predict every railway incident?
No. AI can help identify patterns and prioritise review, but its output depends on available data and validation. It cannot guarantee incident prevention or replace competent safety decisions.

Does digital EHS software automatically ensure compliance?
No. Software can organise obligations, evidence, responsibilities and review dates. Compliance depends on applicable requirements, competent interpretation and effective controls in practice.

What should a project digitise first?
Choose a recurring problem with a clear owner and measurable outcome. Inspection-to-action closure is often a practical pilot because teams can follow a finding from capture through verification.
